  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through Monday with worsening drought\nconditions. Cool today but becoming warmer Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSome areas did see beneficial rain Friday into Friday night,\nwith up to about a third of an inch from Clayton lake southeast\nto Sherman and Topsfield. Other areas generally got a tenth of\nan inch or less. Measurable rain has come to an end, and\ncooler/drier air has moved in.\n\nDry conditions a virtual certainty today, Sunday, and Monday.\nHigh pressure will be centered to our south, so it will remain a\nbit breezy mainly during the day each day. There will be a\npronounced warming trend today through Monday. Highs today \nwill range from around 40 in the far north to the upper 40s \nDowneast. However, Monday we will see temperatures soaring to \naround 60 in the north and 65 Downeast.\n\nFor those venturing outside this (Halloween) evening, there is\nhigh confidence in no precipitation, but it will be cool, with\ntemperatures at sunset in the mid to upper 30s north, and low\n40s Bangor/Downeast. By 9pm, it will fall to the low 30s north \nand mid to upper 30s Bangor/Downeast. Just a light breeze.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return Monday night and remain \nthrough the rest of the week, but very low confidence on whether\nor not there will be notable drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLate Monday night into Tuesday, a 15-30 percent chance of \nprecipitation returns, best shot in the northern areas. If this \nprecipitation occurs Monday night/Tuesday, it would likely be a \nquarter inch or less. Will have to keep an eye on a potential \nbackdoor cold front moving in from the northeast at the same \ntime. Heading into Tuesday night, Wednesday, and beyond, \nforecast confidence drops precipitously. Above average \ntemperatures are likely Wednesday/Thursday, but there is very \nlow confidence in whether or not there will be any rain. Some \nensembles/models have decent rain, while others have none. \nHeading toward Thursday night/Friday, there is a little better \nsignal for a stronger low pressure or cold front and a decent \nrain event. That said, confidence is still very low, as while \nmost models have measurable rain at some point late Thursday \nthrough Friday, there is large variation in how much.",
